Read the local end again once the store is open.

The pusher samples how far local WAL is known good before it asks for
the writer lease, and compares the store's resume point against that
sample after the open returns. Everything between those two moments is
unaccounted for, and on a node that just crashed it is exactly the
window recovery runs in.

A pusher kill drill on server3 caught it. The worker came up with the
postmaster, sampled the replay pointer at 0/BCA5F50 while redo was
still going, and did not come out of the open for 59 seconds. Redo
finished at 0/BE2E6E0 in the meantime, the store legitimately held
0/BE2E028 from the dead session, and RecoveryInProgress was long since
false, so the check fired on a pointer nobody had looked at for a
minute and the worker died. Its replacement started 130 ms later, read
the same store, and re-pushed the difference without a complaint,
which is what should have happened the first time.

The check itself is right: past recovery, a store ahead of the cluster
that owns it is unexplainable and the pusher must not paper over it.
Only the pointer was wrong, so it is read again inside the branch that
uses it. A store with no stream keeps the older sample on purpose,
because that is the one mirroring starts from and moving it forward
would skip whatever was written while the open ran, rather than re-read
it.

Slow opens are a separate problem and stay one; this only stops a slow
open from also costing a worker restart. See zou #375.

diff --git a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
index 1de1789..d3ad9f6 100644
--- a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
@@ -373,6 +373,23 @@ ZouWalPusherMain(Datum main_arg)
 	 */
 	if (resume != 0)
 	{
+		/*
+		 * How far local WAL is known good now, rather than when this
+		 * worker started. Opening the store takes as long as it takes,
+		 * recovery runs while it does, and a pointer sampled before the
+		 * lease was granted can be a minute behind the one replay has
+		 * reached by the time the answer comes back. Held against that
+		 * stale pointer an ordinary re-push reads as a store ahead of
+		 * its own cluster, and the worker dies for it: that is how one
+		 * kill drill cost sixty seconds instead of two (zou #375).
+		 *
+		 * The fresh sample only decides that question. A store with no
+		 * stream keeps the pointer this worker started from, because
+		 * there mirroring begins at `pushed` and anything written
+		 * during the open would be skipped rather than re-read.
+		 */
+		XLogRecPtr	local = zou_wal_local_end(&tli);
+
 		/*
 		 * A store ahead of the cluster it belongs to is nothing local
 		 * can explain, once the cluster is up. During recovery it is
@@ -380,20 +397,20 @@ ZouWalPusherMain(Datum main_arg)
 		 * store already holds what follows, and there is nothing to
 		 * push until replay comes past the end of the stream.
 		 */
-		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume > pushed && !RecoveryInProgress())
+		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume > local && !RecoveryInProgress())
 			ereport(FATAL,
 					(errmsg("zou store holds WAL through %X/%X but local flush is only %X/%X",
 							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS((XLogRecPtr) resume),
-							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(pushed))));
-		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume > pushed)
+							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(local))));
+		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume > local)
 			ereport(LOG,
 					(errmsg("zou wal pusher waiting for recovery to replay past %X/%X, where the stream ends",
 							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS((XLogRecPtr) resume))));
-		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume < pushed)
+		if ((XLogRecPtr) resume < local)
 			ereport(LOG,
 					(errmsg("zou wal pusher re-pushing %X/%X to %X/%X missed by the previous session",
 							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS((XLogRecPtr) resume),
-							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(pushed))));
+							LSN_FORMAT_ARGS(local))));
 		pushed = (XLogRecPtr) resume;
 	}
 
